    {
      "id": "events-through-edges-instructions",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Route Authoring Contract",
      "position": {
        "x": 1040,
        "y": 0
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 260,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "events-through-edges-declaration"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# What the investigation found\n\n- **The root is already an emitter.** It declares graph.structure.changed and GraphEditor announces it by diffing the graph (graphStructureEvents.js).\n- **But it has no event output port.** portDeclarations.js derives event ports only for non-root nodes, so nothing can be drawn from the root today. This is the one real obstacle to putting behaviour on the graph; it is a handle, not a second root Port node.\n- **Delivery follows Handler nodes, not edges.** eventDelivery.js matches Handlers by data.trigger. A Method already runs directly as a handler (GraphEditor invokeHandler), and a Method can be a listens target, so the executor needs no change.\n- **A graph-wide event loops.** A Method that writes its own faces would trigger itself. The chain guard (rootOccurrenceId) has to exist first.\n- **Scale.** 144 Handlers in canon (31 graphs), 54 in the library (14 graphs). 113 and 40 are class-scoped, 2 are bridged, none is in the app.\n- **Two unfinished paths to remove.** The Method's direct trigger (listens into a Method) has an editor but no clear delivery path; it goes.\n\n# The rule\n\nA Method runs only through an invokes edge from an event port in the same graph. Nothing is called by class or from another graph."
        }
      }
    },
